+# Decision: Change Delivery Approach
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event: editors submit commands over HTTPS, and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. How that committed revision reaches connected browsers is a separate question from how it is accepted, and the project's goals bound it from both sides — committed changes must show up in connected browsers within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, the board must stay usable for up to 800 simultaneous browsers with opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second, and a browser that has been disconnected for up to 20 minutes must be able to recover the latest board. The choice also commits the change store, delivery, and browser-sync components to a specific shape: each candidate approach implies a different obligation for how revisions are stored, published, and resumed after a gap.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **Meets the 15-second freshness goal under normal connectivity** (inherited from the HLD's Goals). Any option that cannot meet this is out of contention regardless of its other merits.
+- **Scales to 800 simultaneous browsers and 20-edit/second bursts** (inherited from the HLD's Goals).
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Recurring cost and vendor coupling** (major). The HLD's infrastructure tenet permits both self-hosted and managed infrastructure — neither is gated out — but the project still has to weigh the recurring fee and the dependency a managed option creates against the alternative of owning that work itself.
+- **Connection and reconnect operational burden** (major). Whoever delivers revisions over a live connection also owns connection capacity and reconnect correctness. This is a direct, ongoing operational cost, distinct from the recurring-fee criterion above.
+- **Update latency** (moderate). The HLD states subsecond updates would be useful but are not required, which keeps this below the two major criteria rather than deciding the choice outright.
+- **Implementation complexity of the store and delivery layers** (minor). Each approach pushes a different shape of work onto the change store and delivery components (cursor queries, replayable revisions, or publication reconciliation); none of this work is prohibitive, so it does not drive the decision on its own.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers poll an HTTPS endpoint on a five-second interval and receive any revisions committed since their last-seen cursor. Delivery stays plain request/response — no persistent connection, no session state on the server. The change store needs only cursor-based range queries over its revision history.
+
+- Connection/reconnect burden: **strong fit** — there are no persistent connections or reconnect logic to build; a dropped request simply retries.
+- Recurring cost and vendor coupling: **strong fit** — no vendor, no connection infrastructure beyond the existing HTTPS command path.
+- Update latency: **weak fit** — updates lag by up to five seconds, and every browser issues a request every five seconds regardless of whether anything changed, which is repeated read pressure that scales with browser count rather than with edit rate.
+- Implementation complexity: **strong fit** — cursor queries are the simplest storage shape of the three.
+
+Summary: the cheapest and operationally simplest option, paid for in staleness and in read load that grows with idle browsers rather than with actual activity.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server operates its own WebSocket connections and pushes each committed revision directly to subscribed browsers as it is committed. The change store must keep revisions replayable and publish them reliably to delivery; delivery owns session management for every open socket; browsers must detect disconnects and run catch-up logic to recover any revisions missed while disconnected.
+
+- Connection/reconnect burden: **weak fit** — the project now owns connection capacity for up to 800 concurrent sockets and the correctness of reconnect/catch-up after drops, work that polling and the hosted option both avoid or hand off.
+- Recurring cost and vendor coupling: **strong fit** — no per-connection or per-message vendor fee, and no dependency on a third party's realtime platform or its API surface.
+- Update latency: **strong fit** — revisions are pushed as they commit, well inside the 15-second goal with margin for opening-time bursts.
+- Implementation complexity: **partial fit** — replayable revisions and reliable publication are more storage machinery than cursor queries, but the work stays inside the project's own codebase rather than against a provider's integration surface.
+
+Summary: fast updates with no recurring fee or vendor dependency, in exchange for owning connection scaling and reconnect correctness as a standing operational responsibility.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A third-party realtime pub/sub provider carries the live connections; the server publishes committed revisions into it. The change store needs publication reconciliation (confirming the provider actually delivered what was published); delivery becomes a thin wrapper around the provider's publish API; browsers subscribe using the provider's client library and its own reconnect model.
+
+- Connection/reconnect burden: **strong fit** — the provider owns connection scaling and reconnect handling for all 800 browsers.
+- Recurring cost and vendor coupling: **weak fit** — a recurring per-connection or per-message fee, and the board's delivery path becomes dependent on a provider's availability, API stability, and pricing.
+- Update latency: **strong fit** — comparable to self-hosted sockets, well inside the freshness goal.
+- Implementation complexity: **partial fit** — reconciliation logic and a provider integration replace in-house session management, trading one kind of work for another rather than eliminating it.
+
+Summary: the quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection operations work, paid for in a recurring bill and coupling the board to a specific vendor.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets is the chosen approach. It wins on both major criteria taken together: it carries no recurring vendor fee and no provider coupling, matching the hosted option's latency without the hosted option's ongoing bill or dependency. The cost is accepting weak fit on the connection/reconnect criterion — the project takes on connection capacity and reconnect correctness that either polling or a hosted provider would have absorbed. That trade was taken deliberately: fast, vendor-independent updates were judged worth owning that operational surface, rather than accepting the staleness and read load of polling or the recurring cost and coupling of a hosted provider. The HLD's infrastructure tenet — that self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted — is exercised here in the self-hosted direction; it did not predetermine the choice, since the tenet leaves either direction open.
+
+Committing to this approach obligates the change store to keep revisions replayable and to publish them reliably to delivery, obligates delivery to manage WebSocket sessions for every connected browser, and obligates browser sync to implement reconnect and catch-up logic for recovering missed revisions after a disconnect of up to 20 minutes. It forecloses, at least for the pilot, adopting a hosted realtime provider without reworking all three of those components.
